 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "The standard affine chart in relative projective space",
 "statement": "For every $n$ and $S$, the map induced by\n$T_i\\mapsto T_i/T_0$ identifies $\\mathbb A^n_S$ with the standard open\n$D_+(T_0)\\subseteq\\mathbb P^n_S$. In particular it is an open immersion\nover $S$ and is compatible with base change.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-nagata-compactification/sk-lem-nagata-sketch-affine-cover/lemma-nagata-relative-affine-space-open-immersion.md",
  "place": "node lemma-nagata-relative-affine-space-open-immersion, order 310"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "def-nagata-affine-proj",
  "def-nagata-homogeneous-localization-degree-zero",
  "def-nagata-projective-space-base-change",
  "def-nagata-relative-affine-space",
  "def-nagata-relative-projective-space"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "On $V=\\operatorname{Spec}(R)$, the degree-zero part of\n$R[T_0,\\ldots,T_n]_{T_0}$ is the polynomial ring\n$R[T_1/T_0,\\ldots,T_n/T_0]$. Thus the affine chart is canonically\n$\\operatorname{Spec}(R[T_1,\\ldots,T_n])$. These identifications commute\nwith localization in $R$, so they glue by\n[lemma-nagata-relative-affine-space-charts]; the relative-Proj\nrestriction isomorphisms from\n[lemma-nagata-relative-proj-exists] identify the glued target with\n$D_+(T_0)$. Standard Proj charts are open, proving the assertion.",
   "uses": [
    "lemma-nagata-relative-affine-space-charts",
    "lemma-nagata-relative-proj-exists"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  }
 ],
